# <%~ it.instance.name || it.instance.slug %>: Requisition Brief
<%
  /* `requisition-brief.requires` in definition.yaml is this document's authoring and Gate
     scope, and mostly follows render order; the v3 paragraph below lists where it doesn't.
     `na()` is injected once as an Eta functionHeader by lib/render.js and is in scope here
     without redefinition; it renders a literal "— n/a —" under the heading when a bare
     (gate-blocking) field is blank, so a document is never silently short a section. `?`
     entries are mostly suppress-when-blank `<% if %>` blocks instead, since they never block
     the gate and an empty heading would be noise.

     No approval section, by design. This document is the case put to whoever signs the
     requisition stage off, as the "Approved to Recruit" confirmation; the approval is that
     sign-off. See definition.yaml.

     v2 (#90): `engagement.type` is now a select, `engagement.term` is a new optional prose
     field beside it — see modules/engagement.yaml.

     v3 (#154): `engagement.approval-route` is gone. The standard route is printed from the type
     (Fixed term / Permanent -> full chain; Vendor / Contractor -> shortened), with
     `engagement.route-variation` under it when present. The term heading shows whenever the
     type is not Permanent, with "— not stated —" (not na()'s "— n/a —") when blank, so a
     missing term is visible; the route line likewise reads "— not stated —" until a type is
     chosen. `role-evaluation.method` moved to a closing record section after the outcome.
     `open-questions.process-gaps?` is in `requires` so the requesting department can record a
     gap where it happens; it is printed only in the Hire Record. */
  const reqType = it.modules.engagement.type
  const reqTerm = it.modules.engagement.term
  const reqRoute = (reqType === 'Fixed term' || reqType === 'Permanent') ? 'Full approval chain'
    : (reqType === 'Vendor' || reqType === 'Contractor') ? 'Shortened approval chain' : '— not stated —'
%>
